 ---
 
-When a diff adds a new command to an inline-orchestrator workflow (`.asd/workflows/asd-phase-*.md`), open the matching `.asd/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and check its `claude.allowed-tools` covers it. Most phase skills grant bare `Bash`, but a few (e.g. retro, stack) grant only `Bash(node .asd/runtime.js:*)`, so a new `gh …` step silently falls outside the declared tool policy.
+When a diff adds a new command to an inline-orchestrator workflow (`.asd/workflows/asd-phase-*.md`), open the matching `.asd/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and check its `claude.allowed-tools` covers it. Most phase skills grant bare `Bash`, but a few grant only narrow `Bash(<prefix>:*)` patterns (e.g. stack: only `Bash(node .asd/runtime.js:*)`; retro: that plus its named `gh` calls), so a new command outside those prefixes silently falls outside the declared tool policy.
 
 **Why:** the skill file is usually unchanged and outside the manifest, so the gap is invisible in the diff; an "automatic, no gate" AC then breaks on Claude Code via a permission prompt or denial.
 
diff --git a/.claude/agent-memory/asd-reviewer-combined/feedback_shell-quoting-claims.md b/.claude/agent-memory/asd-reviewer-combined/feedback_shell-quoting-claims.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..ae26c72c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.claude/agent-memory/asd-reviewer-combined/feedback_shell-quoting-claims.md
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+---
+name: shell-quoting-claims
+description: A canon rule claiming a quoted string is safe in both bash and PowerShell — check PowerShell's typographic quotes (U+201C/U+201D/U+201E act as ")
+metadata:
+  type: feedback
+---
+
+When canon or a tech-reference states a character-stripping rule that makes interpolated text "safe" in bash and PowerShell, check the stripped set against both shells: PowerShell treats typographic double quotes “ ” „ (U+201C, U+201D, U+201E) as `"` (Microsoft `about_Quoting_Rules`), and backtick and `$` expand in its double-quoted strings; bash needs backtick, `$`, `"`, `\` (`!` only in interactive shells).
+
+**Why:** Codex on Windows runs PowerShell; consumer-derived text (e.g. a guardrail in a non-English docs language) can carry typographic quotes the ASCII-only strip list misses, ending the string early.
+
+**How to apply:** raise as medium at the rule's line, fix = add the typographic quotes to the removed set. Related: [[new-command-vs-skill-grant]].
